<DocsLayout title="Quick start: shortest paths" kicker="09 / tutorial" description="Build a weighted hopset, then compare its bounded-hop distances with an exact shortest-path baseline.">
  <h2>Make epsilon explicit</h2>
  <p>A hopset is not a magic speedup switch. The useful contract is that the approximation budget is visible at the call site and checked against a baseline for the workload.</p>
  <pre><code>from reachq import WeightedDigraph
from reachq.hopset import build_hopset_for_sssp
from reachq.invariants import assert_distance_approximation

graph = WeightedDigraph()
graph.add_edge("a", "b", 1)
graph.add_edge("b", "c", 2)
graph.add_edge("a", "c", 10)
hopset, beta = build_hopset_for_sssp(graph, epsilon=0.1, random_seed=42)
assert_distance_approximation(
    graph, hopset, source="a", epsilon=0.1, max_hops=20,
)</code></pre>
  <p>The current implementation is sequential. See the <a href="/reachq/benchmarks.html">benchmark guidance</a> for what to record.</p>
</DocsLayout>
